---
import ManualLayout from '../../layouts/ManualLayout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<ManualLayout slug="ai">
  <h2 id="on-device">On-device by default</h2>
  <p>
    The intelligence features use <strong>Apple Intelligence</strong>, running on your own device —
    Mac, iPhone or iPad. Your notes are processed locally and are not sent to us or to any third
    party. These features appear only on hardware where Apple Intelligence is available and enabled
    in System Settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything else in HelloNotes works without them — the AI is an addition, never a requirement.
  </p>

  <h2 id="note-actions">Note actions</h2>
  <p>
    These live in the <strong>Note</strong> menu, beside Rename and Duplicate — not in a panel of
    their own. Each answer appears in the inspector tab that already holds that kind of
    information, so you find it where you would have gone looking anyway. On iPhone and iPad they
    are in the <span aria-hidden="true">✨</span> menu in the toolbar above the note.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Summarise Note</strong> — a short summary of the current note, shown at the top of
      the <strong>Outline</strong> tab. A summary is an outline at a different resolution.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suggest Tags</strong> — proposes tags based on the content, in the{' '}
      <strong>Tags</strong> tab; you choose which to keep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Suggest Links</strong> — finds notes that are plausibly related, in the{' '}
      <strong>References</strong> tab beside the backlinks they would join.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rewrite or Expand Note…</strong> — improve, shorten, simplify, restructure or flesh
      out the note. You see the result and choose <em>Replace Note</em> or <em>Insert Below</em>;
      nothing changes until you do.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Selecting text gives you the actions that need your notes to make sense — link the phrase, find
    related notes, explain it using what you have written. On the Mac they appear in a small
    floating bar; on iPhone and iPad they join the standard selection menu. General rewriting is
    left to <strong>Writing Tools</strong>, which the system already puts in every text view.
  </p>
  <p>
    Can’t remember where a command lives? On the Mac, <kbd>⇧⌘P</kbd> opens a
    <strong>command palette</strong>: type a command’s name to run it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="review-links">Review links</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Note ▸ Review Links…</strong> (<kbd>⇧⌘L</kbd>) walks the note phrase by phrase the way
    a spell check does — <strong>Link</strong>, <strong>Skip</strong> or <strong>Never</strong>. You
    see the phrase in its own sentence and the opening lines of the note it would point at, because
    the same phrase can deserve a link in one paragraph and not the next.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Every phrase that names another note is a candidate. When a note turns up more than ten, the
      ten whose targets are most related to what you are writing are the ones shown — a long note
      can otherwise produce dozens, and a list nobody finishes reading is a list that gets skipped.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Never</strong> is remembered for that collection, so a phrase you have rejected stops
      coming back. It is kept on your device rather than in your notes — a decision you made is not
      something to commit to a shared repository.
    </li>
    <li>Nothing is written until you finish, and the whole set undoes in one step.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This one needs no AI provider at all: it is an exact scan of your own text.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compose">New note from a prompt</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>File ▸ New Note from a Prompt…</strong> (<kbd>⌃⌘N</kbd>) writes a new note, in two
    modes. <strong>Write</strong> composes one from a description.{' '}
    <strong>Research</strong> investigates a question on the web across several angles and lands the answer as a note, with
    its sources gathered at the end and a record of the question it came from.
  </p>
  <p>
    Either way the draft is connected to what you already have: notes you own on the topic are
    offered as link targets, and <strong>every link that comes back is checked against your
    collection</strong>. Anything naming a note that does not exist is turned back into ordinary
    text, and the draft says so. A link that looks real but points nowhere is worse than no link.
  </p>
  <p>
    You read the whole draft — title and body, both editable — before anything is created. Cancel
    leaves nothing behind. The button is <strong>Create Note</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ghost-text">Suggest as I type</h2>
  <p>
    Grey text appears after the cursor when you pause at the end of a line. <kbd>⌥⇥</kbd> or{' '}
    <kbd>→</kbd> accepts it; <kbd>Esc</kbd> dismisses it.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>The suggestion is not part of your note until you accept it.</strong> It is drawn on
      screen and nothing more — it cannot reach a save, a search result, your links or a Git diff.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    It runs <strong>on your device only</strong>: a suggestion that has to cross the network arrives
    after you have already typed past it. Off until you turn it on, in{' '}
    <strong>Assistant Settings ▸ Inline completion</strong>. Mac only for now.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ask">Ask your library</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Ask Library</strong> answers questions using your own notes as the source. It finds the
    relevant ones, reads them, and answers with <strong>citations</strong> back to the notes it drew
    on so you can check the reasoning and jump to the original.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is most useful for questions you know you have written about but cannot place — “what did I
    decide about the pricing model?” rather than general knowledge.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assistant">The Assistant</h2>
  <p>
    The Assistant is a chat window with tools: it can search your collection, read notes, propose
    edits, and — if you enable them — search and fetch from the web.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <strong>Edits always require your approval.</strong> When the Assistant wants to change a
      note, it shows you the exact change first and waits. Nothing is written to disk until you
      approve it.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    <strong>Skills</strong> let you save reusable instructions for repeated jobs, and{' '}
    <strong>deep research</strong> runs a longer multi-step investigation across your notes and,
    optionally, the web.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">Using your own model</h2>
  <p>
    If you would rather not use Apple Intelligence — or your Mac does not support it — you can
    configure a different model in <strong>Settings ▸ AI</strong>.
  </p>
  <p><strong>Local</strong>, nothing leaves your machine:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ollama</strong> — run <code>ollama serve</code>; no key needed.</li>
    <li><strong>LM Studio</strong> — start its local server; no key needed.</li>
    <li><strong>MLX</strong> — runs a downloaded model on Apple silicon.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Cloud</strong>, using your own API key:</p>
  <p>
    Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, Mistral, Groq, OpenRouter, xAI, DeepSeek, Cerebras, Together AI,
    Perplexity and Ollama Cloud are supported. Paste your key in Settings; it is stored in the macOS
    Keychain and never sent to us.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Cloud providers are <strong>off until you configure one</strong>. When you use one, the text
      for that action goes to that provider under your own account and their privacy policy — see
      the <a href={href('/privacy')}>privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2 id="settings">Choosing where each feature runs</h2>
  <p>
    Settings ▸ AI has two separate choices: the <strong>chat provider</strong> (the Assistant, and
    the research behind New Note from a Prompt) and the <strong>intelligence provider</strong>
    (Summarise, Suggest Tags/Links, Rewrite, Ask Library and typing suggestions). The intelligence
    provider defaults to on-device Apple Intelligence. The chat provider is set to OpenAI, but like
    every cloud provider it stays switched off until you enable it and add a key — so no note text is
    sent to a model until you choose a cloud provider and set it up. <strong>Creativity</strong> controls how varied
    the output is — lower is more focused and predictable.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same settings say what your chosen model can actually do: whether it runs on your device or
    in the cloud, roughly how much of a note it can read at once, and which features would work
    better on a model with more room. An on-device model reads a few thousand characters, so Ask
    Library over a long note is a different experience there than on a large cloud model — worth
    knowing before concluding the feature is poor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="models">Picking a model, and its limits</h2>
  <p>
    Each provider has a <strong>Refresh</strong> button beside its model field. It asks the provider
    which models your key can actually reach, so the list is the current one rather than whatever
    was current when the app was built. <strong>Available models</strong> then lists them, with each
    one’s context size where the provider reports it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can still type any model ID by hand. A fine-tune or a deployment alias that the listing
    doesn’t mention works exactly as before — refreshing never overwrites a model you chose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Eight providers — Gemini, Anthropic, OpenRouter, Mistral, Groq, Together AI, Ollama and LM Studio
    — also report how much each model can hold, and HelloNotes uses that number directly. So a model
    with a million-token window is read as a model with a million-token window, rather than being
    held to a cautious default. OpenAI, xAI, DeepSeek, Cerebras and Perplexity publish model names
    but not limits; for those the app keeps a conservative default and says so rather than guessing.
  </p>

  <h3 id="parameters">Setting the limits yourself</h3>
  <p>Below the model, three settings that apply to that provider alone:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Temperature</strong> — the same idea as Creativity, but per provider and over the range
      that provider genuinely accepts. Anthropic stops at 1.0; most others go to 2.0. Leave it and
      the Creativity slider applies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Context budget</strong> — how much note text is sent per request, in characters. Blank
      uses the model’s reported limit. Lower it to spend less.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Max reply length</strong> — a cap on the generated answer, in tokens. Blank lets the
      provider decide.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    All of this is in one place on both platforms: <strong>Settings ▸ AI</strong> on the Mac, and{' '}
    <strong>Settings ▸ AI ▸ Providers &amp; API Keys</strong> on iPad and iPhone.
  </p>
</ManualLayout>
